  "docs": {
    "purpose": "Gives an admin a deep diagnostic view of one lead — a building permit or a committee-of-adjustment application — showing every input that fed the cost model, its trades, its lifecycle timeline, and any linked application on the other track, so staff can debug or QA why the system produced the numbers it did.",
    "data_story": "It reads 20 tables — `permits`, `cost_estimates`, `permit_parcels`, `parcels`, `parcel_buildings`, `building_footprints`, `neighbourhoods`, `scope_intensity_matrix`, `lead_views`, `permit_trades`, `trades`, `trade_forecasts`, `entities`, `logic_variables`, `permit_phase_transitions`, `phase_stay_calibration`, `coa_applications`, `universal_stream_catalog`, `lifecycle_status_history` and `lead_trades` and writes nothing. Its response is an explicit pick-by-name projection of 13 fields, so a column added to a table upstream does not leak into the client by accident. It is called from 1 place: `src/features/admin-flight-center/api/useLeadInspect.ts`.",
    "under_126": "Becomes a **CONTRACT descriptor** of archetype `QUERY`. It declares its `database.reads[]` with named columns, its response projection as an explicit whitelist, its auth class, its rate bucket and its staleness — and `outputs.writes` is forced to `\"none\"`. Its `consumers[]` becomes the edge list of the fan-out graph — the join nothing in this estate holds today."
  },
